WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:03.520 --> 00:00:04.980
Welcome to "Ear Biscuits,"

00:00:04.980 --> 00:00:07.050
the podcast where two lifelong friends

00:00:07.050 --> 00:00:09.380
talk about life for a long time.

00:00:09.380 --> 00:00:10.213
I'm Rhett.

00:00:10.213 --> 00:00:11.150
And I'm Link.

00:00:11.150 --> 00:00:14.280
This week at the round
table of dim lighting,

00:00:14.280 --> 00:00:15.590
you are in for a treat.

00:00:15.590 --> 00:00:17.020
Ooh, I'm excited about this.

00:00:17.020 --> 00:00:18.820
I've been looking forward to this one.

00:00:18.820 --> 00:00:21.560
We want to introduce you

00:00:21.560 --> 00:00:26.390
to our other best friend in college.

00:00:26.390 --> 00:00:28.040
Who we've kept in touch with.

00:00:28.040 --> 00:00:29.270
I mean, we'll get into some of that,

00:00:29.270 --> 00:00:30.780
but we wanna introduce you

00:00:30.780 --> 00:00:34.980
to the one, the only Gregg Hartsfield.

00:00:34.980 --> 00:00:36.980
Gregg you're here. Well, no, you're not.

00:00:36.980 --> 00:00:38.671
Hey, I made it.

00:00:38.671 --> 00:00:39.504
You made it but you're--

00:00:39.504 --> 00:00:42.850
You made it to your home and a phone

00:00:42.850 --> 00:00:43.980
that you're communicating through.

00:00:43.980 --> 00:00:47.110
And you're in Washington State.

00:00:47.110 --> 00:00:51.010
Yes, I'm there, that's where is here.

00:00:51.010 --> 00:00:52.910
You're still in Burbank.

00:00:52.910 --> 00:00:54.410
No, we've talked about Gregg

00:00:55.300 --> 00:00:56.970
a lot throughout the years.

00:00:56.970 --> 00:00:59.527
I mean, most recently, if
you watch our other show,

00:00:59.527 --> 00:01:03.740
"Good Mythical Morning,"
you know that the black bass

00:01:03.740 --> 00:01:07.800
that was on the set for
basically many years.

00:01:07.800 --> 00:01:08.633
Basically.

00:01:08.633 --> 00:01:10.690
Most of the history of the show,

00:01:10.690 --> 00:01:12.810
was a bass that Gregg gave us

00:01:12.810 --> 00:01:16.370
when he left to go to
Argentina right after college.

00:01:16.370 --> 00:01:18.880
So we are talking of like 20 years ago.

00:01:18.880 --> 00:01:19.900
So we had the bass.

00:01:19.900 --> 00:01:23.760
The bass was played in a few
early Rhett and Link songs

00:01:23.760 --> 00:01:25.720
when I would like lay down a baseline.

00:01:25.720 --> 00:01:27.840
We later realized that I
could just play the guitar,

00:01:27.840 --> 00:01:30.110
and then we would just it
lower an octave in garage band.

00:01:30.110 --> 00:01:31.203
Yeah. Yeah.

00:01:32.160 --> 00:01:36.540
But recently, Gregg
asked for the bass back,

00:01:36.540 --> 00:01:38.260
and so that whole story is unfolding

00:01:38.260 --> 00:01:40.072
on "Good Mythical Morning," but--

00:01:40.072 --> 00:01:43.090
So, by the way, if you have something

00:01:43.090 --> 00:01:46.240
that you think can fit in that space

00:01:46.240 --> 00:01:50.490
that the base was,
occupied, you let us know.

00:01:50.490 --> 00:01:51.323
There's a Twitter post.

00:01:51.323 --> 00:01:56.323
[Link] You post your photo and,
basically, your sales pitch

00:01:56.580 --> 00:01:58.680
as to why this thing is special.

00:01:58.680 --> 00:01:59.840
Hopefully, it's the base.

00:01:59.840 --> 00:02:04.080
I'm trying to lobby for
the bases for Alabama.

00:02:04.080 --> 00:02:04.913
Yeah.

00:02:04.913 --> 00:02:05.750
To donate one of his bases.

00:02:05.750 --> 00:02:06.917
I just think that would be funny.

00:02:06.917 --> 00:02:09.890
And if he won't, I'm just
hoping for a guy named Gregg,

00:02:09.890 --> 00:02:13.280
preferably a guy named Gregg
with two G's at the end.

00:02:13.280 --> 00:02:16.410
So, Gregg, have you
received the base back?

00:02:16.410 --> 00:02:17.734
It's here. It's here.

00:02:17.734 --> 00:02:20.657
It needs to be restrung.

00:02:20.657 --> 00:02:22.830
You have not maintained the base.

00:02:22.830 --> 00:02:24.430
It wasn't broken when it arrived.

00:02:24.430 --> 00:02:26.530
It just needs to be refreshed.

00:02:26.530 --> 00:02:30.400
Yes. It needs a light
buffing and some new strings.

00:02:30.400 --> 00:02:34.680
Yeah. I mean, base strings
do not last two decades.

00:02:34.680 --> 00:02:38.340
They probably last like,
You might get, I don't know,

00:02:38.340 --> 00:02:39.950
six months outta some base strings?

00:02:39.950 --> 00:02:41.790
Now listen, if you don't
replace these strings

00:02:41.790 --> 00:02:44.750
and play this thing within
like the next two weeks,

00:02:44.750 --> 00:02:48.770
you're at risk of us
asking you years from now

00:02:48.770 --> 00:02:51.230
if you played it, and
you'll be embarrassed.

00:02:51.230 --> 00:02:53.400
You'll have to start lying
to us that you're playing it.

00:02:53.400 --> 00:02:55.820
So you need to get on it, man.

00:02:55.820 --> 00:02:57.303
I'm working on the plan.

00:02:58.555 --> 00:03:02.190
I didn't have a plan till just now,

00:03:02.190 --> 00:03:03.323
so now I have a plan.

00:03:05.394 --> 00:03:06.873
I don't really know the
details of the plan,

00:03:06.873 --> 00:03:07.971
but there's a plan.

00:03:07.971 --> 00:03:08.804
There's a plan.

00:03:08.804 --> 00:03:11.010
How good did you get it bass
before you gave us that bass?

00:03:11.010 --> 00:03:12.833
I was not so good.

00:03:15.290 --> 00:03:18.040
From one to 10, I might be a 2.

00:03:18.040 --> 00:03:18.873
Okay. Right.

00:03:18.873 --> 00:03:19.706
It's fine.

00:03:19.706 --> 00:03:20.830
You've been holding steady at a two.

00:03:20.830 --> 00:03:24.680
I did go to a jazz club in Raleigh

00:03:24.680 --> 00:03:27.464
in Open Mic Night, one time,

00:03:27.464 --> 00:03:30.780
and for some reason, people
thought I played bass well,

00:03:30.780 --> 00:03:32.021
but I don't think I did.

00:03:32.021 --> 00:03:33.960
Are you talking about
when you were in college?

00:03:33.960 --> 00:03:36.150
No, that was I after college.

00:03:36.150 --> 00:03:39.121
I went with Tom from our church.

00:03:39.121 --> 00:03:41.770
Okay. And you did a little bass thing.

00:03:41.770 --> 00:03:43.500
Man, I didn't know you were moonlighting.

00:03:43.500 --> 00:03:45.570
I do remember that you
had an acoustic guitar

00:03:45.570 --> 00:03:47.270
when we first met.

00:03:47.270 --> 00:03:50.510
I think you could play some
Grateful Dead songs, but...

00:03:50.510 --> 00:03:52.600
All right. So tell us,

00:03:52.600 --> 00:03:55.810
what do you remember about
meeting us for the first time?

00:03:55.810 --> 00:03:59.450
So I've spent the last 30 minutes

00:03:59.450 --> 00:04:03.290
trying to recreate this memory in my mind.

00:04:03.290 --> 00:04:08.290
Nothing really pops out,
When I first met you guys,

00:04:09.890 --> 00:04:13.853
I think it was a freshman year of college,

00:04:15.244 --> 00:04:20.244
and I saw, read up on the stage a crew,

00:04:20.380 --> 00:04:21.453
and I was like,

00:04:22.387 --> 00:04:23.600
"There's that guy up there."

00:04:23.600 --> 00:04:25.470
That didn't really care.

00:04:25.470 --> 00:04:27.560
Didn't really even want
to be at the meeting.

00:04:27.560 --> 00:04:30.370
We really didn't meet, in my recollection,

00:04:30.370 --> 00:04:31.830
until sophomore year,

00:04:31.830 --> 00:04:34.130
so, well, if that was the
first time you saw me,

00:04:34.130 --> 00:04:35.380
that was your sophomore year?

00:04:35.380 --> 00:04:37.940
Because I may have gotten
up front freshman year

00:04:37.940 --> 00:04:40.310
for something, but I
wasn't emceeing until,

00:04:40.310 --> 00:04:44.140
because I lobbied to
emcee in my freshman year,

00:04:44.140 --> 00:04:46.740
knowing that the guy who
was a senior was graduating.

00:04:46.740 --> 00:04:47.610
Oh, okay.

00:04:47.610 --> 00:04:49.273
So I talked to Mark and was like,

00:04:49.273 --> 00:04:52.987
"Can I emcee next year?"

00:04:54.370 --> 00:04:57.570
Because I remember it being the
beginning of sophomore year.

00:04:57.570 --> 00:04:59.900
That was definitely
when we became friends.

00:04:59.900 --> 00:05:01.843
Pretty early on sophomore year.

00:05:03.630 --> 00:05:05.833
But what was your impression of us?

00:05:05.833 --> 00:05:08.130
I mean, I remember my impression of you,

00:05:08.130 --> 00:05:09.540
but I'll let you go first.

00:05:09.540 --> 00:05:10.630
Whatever you remember

00:05:10.630 --> 00:05:14.180
of when we first started
becoming friends, sophomore year.

00:05:14.180 --> 00:05:16.580
It was within the context
of the campus crusade.

00:05:18.260 --> 00:05:23.110
Todd Smith, who was, and still
is a full-time staff member,

00:05:23.110 --> 00:05:25.930
he was leading Bible
studies and organizing

00:05:25.930 --> 00:05:30.220
the weekly meeting and a lot
of the organizational stuff

00:05:30.220 --> 00:05:33.870
that happened on campus
with the organization.

00:05:33.870 --> 00:05:37.500
But he knew you, and then he knew us,

00:05:37.500 --> 00:05:40.460
and then we must have
met through Todd. Right?

00:05:40.460 --> 00:05:41.700
I think so. I think so.

00:05:41.700 --> 00:05:44.440
But I don't recall.

00:05:44.440 --> 00:05:47.113
I know that like we hit it off.

00:05:48.230 --> 00:05:50.130
We hung out all the time.

00:05:50.130 --> 00:05:51.437
Yeah, we were fast friends.

00:05:51.437 --> 00:05:53.510
I really enjoyed hanging
out with you guys.

00:05:53.510 --> 00:05:56.093
So the thing that I remember is,

00:05:57.270 --> 00:06:00.550
I mean, I don't remember
the specific thing,

00:06:00.550 --> 00:06:02.520
but I have to know that
our first impression

00:06:02.520 --> 00:06:07.520
of Gregg was, "This dude is different."

00:06:07.812 --> 00:06:11.410
I mean, and take this in a positive way,

00:06:11.410 --> 00:06:16.410
but like, this dude is strange and funny

00:06:16.700 --> 00:06:19.020
and he's just different
than anybody else we knew,

00:06:19.020 --> 00:06:20.913
so we were very much drawn to,

00:06:23.900 --> 00:06:24.977
everybody in campus crusade, I don't know,

00:06:24.977 --> 00:06:28.130
it's just like everybody
fit into a certain mold.

00:06:28.130 --> 00:06:28.963
Well, I mean--

00:06:28.963 --> 00:06:29.796
But Gregg, you stood out.

00:06:29.796 --> 00:06:32.803
You were like this super skinny dude

00:06:32.803 --> 00:06:35.460
with these real floppy clothes, like us.

00:06:35.460 --> 00:06:36.293
You were dressed how we-

00:06:36.293 --> 00:06:37.710
These huge jeans.
That's the thing.

00:06:37.710 --> 00:06:41.970
So we were at NC State, which is already,

00:06:41.970 --> 00:06:43.270
it's a technical school, right?

00:06:43.270 --> 00:06:45.850
It's a technical state
school in the south,

00:06:45.850 --> 00:06:47.830
and we were also then sort of filtered

00:06:47.830 --> 00:06:50.660
into this Christian group.

00:06:50.660 --> 00:06:53.143
So there was a little more,

00:06:54.160 --> 00:06:58.770
for lack of a better
word, Southern preppiness.

00:06:58.770 --> 00:06:59.603
So--
Right.

00:06:59.603 --> 00:07:00.820
So there's a certain, I don't know,

00:07:00.820 --> 00:07:01.653
if you grew up in the south,

00:07:01.653 --> 00:07:03.110
you know exactly what I'm talking about,

00:07:03.110 --> 00:07:07.370
but a guy who will wear a
polo shirt and a trucker hat.

00:07:09.170 --> 00:07:10.350
It was a little frat-ish-

00:07:10.350 --> 00:07:12.560
It's a little fratty.
But not full on frat.

00:07:12.560 --> 00:07:15.450
So you're not quite redneck
and not quite preppy,

00:07:15.450 --> 00:07:17.680
and that was most of the people.

00:07:17.680 --> 00:07:21.430
Now we kinda came in thinking
that we were rock stars,

00:07:21.430 --> 00:07:23.230
we're dyeing our hair, bleaching our hair,

00:07:23.230 --> 00:07:24.730
wearing these big baggy pants.

00:07:26.210 --> 00:07:28.070
Embracing the alternative '90s scene.

00:07:28.070 --> 00:07:30.230
So we were really leaning hard into that,

00:07:30.230 --> 00:07:31.840
and it wasn't difficult to stand out

00:07:31.840 --> 00:07:34.390
because there wasn't a lot
of people in our small circle

00:07:34.390 --> 00:07:35.223
doing that,

00:07:35.223 --> 00:07:38.387
and then Gregg comes along,
and it was immediately like,

00:07:38.387 --> 00:07:40.677
"Oh, this guy, he feels like

00:07:40.677 --> 00:07:43.000
"he's on a very similar wavelength."

00:07:43.000 --> 00:07:48.000
I mean, and we thought
that you were a stoner.

00:07:49.510 --> 00:07:53.018
In like a really, you had this delivery,

00:07:53.018 --> 00:07:55.100
I remember whenever we would see

00:07:55.100 --> 00:07:56.887
Gregg would be like, "What's up?"

00:07:57.888 --> 00:07:58.721
I was kind of stoner.

00:07:59.760 --> 00:08:00.593
Okay. Yeah.

00:08:00.593 --> 00:08:03.600
So there was some recovering
stoner thing happening.

00:08:03.600 --> 00:08:05.500
So by the time we met you, you were like,

00:08:05.500 --> 00:08:08.240
yeah, you were being introduced

00:08:08.240 --> 00:08:11.890
to the straight narrow
through campus crusade,

00:08:11.890 --> 00:08:15.890
and we were, Todd was trying to bring you

00:08:15.890 --> 00:08:17.800
into the fold basically, right?

00:08:17.800 --> 00:08:19.030
Yeah.

00:08:19.030 --> 00:08:22.243
He was putting in me in
my, in the right place.

00:08:23.200 --> 00:08:26.982
Yeah. And when he
introduced us, I was like,

00:08:26.982 --> 00:08:31.920
we just found you very
funny, very entertaining.

00:08:31.920 --> 00:08:34.240
Whenever we would say, "What's up?"

00:08:34.240 --> 00:08:35.437
You remember what Gregg would say?

00:08:35.437 --> 00:08:36.270
"Good."

00:08:36.270 --> 00:08:37.103
"Good."

00:08:38.063 --> 00:08:40.280
It's just like anybody who, that's what,

00:08:40.280 --> 00:08:44.490
nobody's response to,
"What's up?" Is, "Good."

00:08:44.490 --> 00:08:46.550
And every time. And you were always good.

00:08:46.550 --> 00:08:47.980
You were always good.

00:08:47.980 --> 00:08:50.810
How else do you respond to that question?

00:08:50.810 --> 00:08:54.100
Well, you want me to
break it down logically?

00:08:54.100 --> 00:08:56.193
Because when you say, "What's up?"

00:08:56.193 --> 00:08:58.929
What you're asking is what is up?

00:08:58.929 --> 00:09:00.554
Like what's going on?

00:09:00.554 --> 00:09:02.470
You're asking me, "How are you doing?"

00:09:02.470 --> 00:09:04.340
Well, I mean, you gotta cut to the chase.

00:09:04.340 --> 00:09:05.390
If you say, "What's up?"

00:09:05.390 --> 00:09:08.690
You could respond with, "Well, I'm good."

00:09:08.690 --> 00:09:10.000
That's one of the things that's up,

00:09:10.000 --> 00:09:11.497
but you were just like, "Good."

00:09:11.497 --> 00:09:15.272
"Nothing much, man." I'm headed to class.

00:09:15.272 --> 00:09:16.420
I'm going back to my dorm.

00:09:16.420 --> 00:09:18.520
You want come over and
play some Twisted Metal?

00:09:18.520 --> 00:09:22.060
Or actually, really,
proper response at that age

00:09:22.060 --> 00:09:25.050
with a familiar person to,
"What's up?" Is, "What's up?"

00:09:25.050 --> 00:09:25.883
Yeah.

00:09:25.883 --> 00:09:27.150
Just say it back, but--

00:09:27.150 --> 00:09:30.700
No one, before, during, or since,

00:09:30.700 --> 00:09:33.840
said, "Good," and that's why we knew

00:09:33.840 --> 00:09:37.480
that you had to be a
constant part of our lives.

00:09:37.480 --> 00:09:41.420
I think that we were in this place,

00:09:41.420 --> 00:09:43.590
we had gone through our
freshman year together.

00:09:43.590 --> 00:09:45.910
We had made a lot of friends.

00:09:45.910 --> 00:09:50.910
We hadn't really, really found our group.

00:09:51.760 --> 00:09:54.720
It was just the two of us on the weekends.

00:09:54.720 --> 00:09:57.800
I was going back home
to see my girlfriend.

00:09:57.800 --> 00:10:01.390
We were still involved in
our band, The Wax Paper Dogs.

00:10:01.390 --> 00:10:04.170
So freshman year was like
this transition period,

00:10:04.170 --> 00:10:07.940
but sophomore year, we
had basically decided

00:10:07.940 --> 00:10:10.510
we were deciding to quit the band.

00:10:10.510 --> 00:10:13.770
You were deciding and
lobbying to be the MC

00:10:13.770 --> 00:10:15.790
of campus crusade's weekly meeting.

00:10:15.790 --> 00:10:16.623
Yeah.

00:10:16.623 --> 00:10:19.760
I was a already involved in the music team

00:10:19.760 --> 00:10:20.790
of the weekly meeting.

00:10:20.790 --> 00:10:25.530
So I was upfront singing,
leading the music.

00:10:25.530 --> 00:10:28.170
So I was like, we were
moving in these positions

00:10:28.170 --> 00:10:31.490
where we were visible positions early on,

00:10:31.490 --> 00:10:34.460
and so college for us, at the
beginning of sophomore year

00:10:34.460 --> 00:10:37.833
was, okay, I've broken
up with my girlfriend,

00:10:39.710 --> 00:10:41.730
now, it's like all in on college

00:10:41.730 --> 00:10:45.800
and right about that same
time, very early on, we meet,

00:10:45.800 --> 00:10:50.800
and it was just like, it was
always fun to have you around.

00:10:52.050 --> 00:10:53.363
So it was just like,

00:10:55.147 --> 00:10:58.917
In a lot of ways, we became
this trio very quickly.

00:11:01.156 --> 00:11:04.160
Do you remember any more
of what you thought of us?

00:11:04.160 --> 00:11:05.630
I'm not fishing for a compliment,

00:11:05.630 --> 00:11:07.840
but I told you that I thought

00:11:07.840 --> 00:11:11.123
you were basically gloriously weird,

00:11:11.123 --> 00:11:13.330
and it was like, unlike anybody else.

00:11:13.330 --> 00:11:16.470
So I was like, let's do this.

00:11:16.470 --> 00:11:19.941
I just thought you guys were my friends.

00:11:19.941 --> 00:11:24.941
I just wanted to, In my life,
I haven't had a ton of friends

00:11:26.070 --> 00:11:26.903
up to that point.

00:11:26.903 --> 00:11:29.460
And I meet you two, and I
was like, this is my people.

00:11:29.460 --> 00:11:33.003
This is who I wanna hang out with.

00:11:33.850 --> 00:11:38.260
I mean, you seemed like
you were in Wood dorm,

00:11:38.260 --> 00:11:40.800
which was like halfway across campus

00:11:40.800 --> 00:11:43.140
in a weird spot where
we didn't know anybody,

00:11:43.140 --> 00:11:44.420
and it was a suite.

00:11:44.420 --> 00:11:48.360
And you spent a lot of time
with Jack freshman year.

00:11:48.360 --> 00:11:51.380
You had a number of friends
that were suite mates, right?

00:11:51.380 --> 00:11:52.863
Yeah, I had... Yeah. Yep.

00:11:52.863 --> 00:11:55.283
And so 'cause it, now
that I think about it,

00:11:55.283 --> 00:11:56.560
because junior year,

00:11:56.560 --> 00:12:00.230
which we'll get to in a
second, we moved off campus,

00:12:00.230 --> 00:12:02.490
because we met at the
beginning of sophomore year,

00:12:02.490 --> 00:12:04.730
it turns out that a lot
happened during sophomore year,

00:12:04.730 --> 00:12:07.670
because sophomore year was
our last year in Sime dorm,

00:12:07.670 --> 00:12:11.310
so that was when Sime dorm, room 24,

00:12:11.310 --> 00:12:14.220
which has since been turned
into a storage closet,

00:12:14.220 --> 00:12:17.650
because the window opens
up underneath the stairs.

00:12:17.650 --> 00:12:22.263
But that was the year that
we got into smoking cigars.

00:12:23.210 --> 00:12:24.950
We would crawl out through that window.

00:12:24.950 --> 00:12:26.270
In that little area.

00:12:26.270 --> 00:12:28.100
I have fond memories of that window.

00:12:28.100 --> 00:12:29.400
Tell us what you remember.

00:12:29.400 --> 00:12:33.032
Well, I remember we would smoke cigars.

00:12:33.032 --> 00:12:35.033
Did we get into the pipes
by that point? I don't know.

00:12:35.033 --> 00:12:36.770
Yes. Pipes by George. Yes.

00:12:36.770 --> 00:12:39.020
Oh, it was low.

00:12:39.020 --> 00:12:39.960
There were some chairs.

00:12:39.960 --> 00:12:44.960
It was chairs permanently staged
out under the stairs area,

00:12:45.833 --> 00:12:48.029
and it was a dirt floor
and it was dark and damp.

00:12:48.029 --> 00:12:48.862
It was.

00:12:48.862 --> 00:12:52.740
Yeah, because it wasn't good
for our respiratory health.

00:12:52.740 --> 00:12:54.210
The only window for our dorm room--

00:12:54.210 --> 00:12:56.541
You had people walking, out of the Sime,

00:12:56.541 --> 00:12:59.100
you can hear them walking out talking.

00:12:59.100 --> 00:13:01.270
Right. Because the main entrance to Sime

00:13:01.270 --> 00:13:05.890
was these huge cement stairs.

00:13:05.890 --> 00:13:09.193
And then underneath that staircase was,

00:13:10.210 --> 00:13:14.320
it was just open air, but
that's where our window was.

00:13:14.320 --> 00:13:16.670
So our window never got sunlight,

00:13:16.670 --> 00:13:18.750
and we would open the window,

00:13:18.750 --> 00:13:21.870
and we would crawl out of our window

00:13:21.870 --> 00:13:24.100
into underneath the stairs

00:13:24.100 --> 00:13:25.930
where everybody's walking
into the first floor.

00:13:25.930 --> 00:13:27.890
And there's bushes on both sides.

00:13:27.890 --> 00:13:30.400
So it was like completely
walled in, it was like a cave.

00:13:30.400 --> 00:13:32.510
You couldn't, you probably could have,

00:13:32.510 --> 00:13:35.440
but you didn't go from
underneath the stairs outside.

00:13:35.440 --> 00:13:36.728
No, we never did that.

00:13:36.728 --> 00:13:38.150
It was enclosed.

00:13:38.150 --> 00:13:41.580
I'm so sad that they made that
into a, not a room anymore

00:13:41.580 --> 00:13:42.860
because it was the best room.

00:13:42.860 --> 00:13:45.040
It was great to be a college
student in there because--

00:13:45.040 --> 00:13:46.110
Students don't need sunlight.

00:13:46.110 --> 00:13:48.310
No matter what time of day
it was, you could sleep.

00:13:48.310 --> 00:13:52.150
It was like having room-darkening blinds,

00:13:52.150 --> 00:13:54.730
but it was just the staircase.

00:13:54.730 --> 00:13:57.210
And I remember, yeah,

00:13:57.210 --> 00:14:00.300
if you didn't get us into
cigars, I don't know who did,

00:14:00.300 --> 00:14:02.237
but what would happen was you would say,

00:14:02.237 --> 00:14:05.750
"Hey, let's hang out,"
and we'll smoke cigars

00:14:05.750 --> 00:14:06.770
and you would come over.

00:14:06.770 --> 00:14:07.760
Backwoods.

00:14:07.760 --> 00:14:08.593
Backwoods.

00:14:08.593 --> 00:14:09.426
We started with Backwoods.

00:14:09.426 --> 00:14:10.756
We were not doing pipes at that time.

00:14:10.756 --> 00:14:12.150
Well, that's 'cause we
didn't have a lot of money.

00:14:12.150 --> 00:14:14.887
I do remember we would
save up and you were like,

00:14:14.887 --> 00:14:18.110
"We need to go to this place
called Pipes by George."

00:14:18.110 --> 00:14:19.750
And we would go down
on Hillsborough Street.

00:14:19.750 --> 00:14:21.020
It's still there.

00:14:21.020 --> 00:14:22.100
Oh, you checked last time you went?

00:14:22.100 --> 00:14:23.320
Yeah. It's still there.

00:14:23.320 --> 00:14:27.610
And you knew George because
you had been in there.

00:14:27.610 --> 00:14:30.493
Yep. Was first name basis with George.

00:14:31.460 --> 00:14:34.350
And you remember what
his catch phrase was?

00:14:34.350 --> 00:14:37.917
No. Well, he would say, packing the pipe,

00:14:37.917 --> 00:14:41.097
"Pack it like a baby, pack it like a mama,

00:14:41.097 --> 00:14:43.293
"and, pack it like a daddy."
or something like that.

00:14:44.500 --> 00:14:46.180
But when you ask them about like,

00:14:46.180 --> 00:14:47.487
what do you think about this one?

00:14:47.487 --> 00:14:48.820
Oh smooth. This is--

00:14:48.820 --> 00:14:49.764
Yeah. They were all--

00:14:49.764 --> 00:14:50.597
Smooth.

00:14:50.597 --> 00:14:51.570
It's real smooth.
It's real smooth.

00:14:51.570 --> 00:14:52.687
Everything. I was like,

00:14:52.687 --> 00:14:55.753
"George, do you have a
tobacco that's not smooth?"

00:14:55.753 --> 00:14:57.847
"No. I specialize in smooth."

00:14:58.900 --> 00:15:00.950
So we would go in there,
and we would buy cigars,

00:15:00.950 --> 00:15:03.340
and we'd save up money to
get like the nicer ones.

00:15:03.340 --> 00:15:04.410
Like a $7 cigar.

00:15:04.410 --> 00:15:05.243
And I remember--

00:15:05.243 --> 00:15:06.076
That was a big Friday night.

00:15:06.076 --> 00:15:07.760
We would go sit underneath
the stoop, and you're right.

00:15:07.760 --> 00:15:09.840
There was dirt. It was a dirt floor.

00:15:09.840 --> 00:15:12.590
It was dank and dark under there.

00:15:12.590 --> 00:15:17.337
And one day, I mean, you
would call and be like,

00:15:17.337 --> 00:15:18.600
"I'm coming over."

00:15:18.600 --> 00:15:23.600
And then he came over and
Gregg was dragging a chair.

00:15:23.780 --> 00:15:26.200
and we were like, "Where did you get this?

00:15:26.200 --> 00:15:30.127
This looks like one of the
chairs from the student center."

00:15:31.160 --> 00:15:32.330
Do you remember that?

00:15:32.330 --> 00:15:33.840
Yeah, I remember now. Yeah.

00:15:33.840 --> 00:15:35.610
I wouldn't have remembered
that, but yeah. Yeah.

00:15:35.610 --> 00:15:37.298
I Brought a chair over.

00:15:37.298 --> 00:15:39.500
You borrowed it, permanent.

00:15:39.500 --> 00:15:41.040
Hold on. He was a student.

00:15:41.040 --> 00:15:41.873
He was a student.

00:15:41.873 --> 00:15:43.760
I mean, he's paying
tuition. It's his chair.

00:15:43.760 --> 00:15:45.810
Just because it's not
currently in the student center

00:15:45.810 --> 00:15:47.020
doesn't mean he can't use it.

00:15:47.020 --> 00:15:47.980
He didn't take it off campus.

00:15:47.980 --> 00:15:50.040
He just brought it to the
little hole under the stairs.

00:15:50.040 --> 00:15:52.120
That was our level of subversion.

00:15:52.120 --> 00:15:55.200
He was like, we were so by the book,

00:15:55.200 --> 00:15:57.570
and I'm talking capital B book here,

00:15:57.570 --> 00:16:00.700
and I mean, Gregg, you were very much,

00:16:00.700 --> 00:16:03.490
do you remember us being like very pious?

00:16:03.490 --> 00:16:05.357
But then, I guess to finish my point,

00:16:05.357 --> 00:16:06.190
then you can answer the question

00:16:06.190 --> 00:16:09.440
is whenever he would come
over and he'd stolen a chair,

00:16:09.440 --> 00:16:12.220
and we're smoking cigars
and we're just blowing

00:16:12.220 --> 00:16:14.700
all the smoke around the entrance to the,

00:16:14.700 --> 00:16:19.410
I just felt like we were
being, well, we were edgy.

00:16:19.410 --> 00:16:22.060
I think I wouldn't say that we were pious,

00:16:22.060 --> 00:16:23.040
we were zealous.

00:16:23.040 --> 00:16:25.140
There's a very distinct
difference between those things.

00:16:25.140 --> 00:16:30.140
Pious is someone who is
obsessed with rule following.

00:16:30.230 --> 00:16:32.600
Now, we were good guys and everything.

00:16:32.600 --> 00:16:34.010
But we were zealous.

00:16:34.010 --> 00:16:35.160
It's more about belief.

00:16:35.160 --> 00:16:37.100
About our belief and our faith, but the--

00:16:37.100 --> 00:16:37.933
I so rule.

00:16:37.933 --> 00:16:42.700
I remember that time being the first time,

00:16:42.700 --> 00:16:43.880
not that we didn't do this before,

00:16:43.880 --> 00:16:47.420
but when you're sitting under
the stairs, smoking a cigar

00:16:47.420 --> 00:16:51.370
is when you start just
talking about stuff,

00:16:51.370 --> 00:16:53.960
and considering a night out,

00:16:53.960 --> 00:16:56.650
just a three hour
conversation about something.

00:16:56.650 --> 00:16:58.460
Yeah. Every weekend.

00:16:58.460 --> 00:17:00.920
That was the beginning
of that being a staple,

00:17:00.920 --> 00:17:02.700
because we weren't going out and partying.

00:17:02.700 --> 00:17:03.730
Nope.

00:17:03.730 --> 00:17:05.810
Do you remember us being zealous?

00:17:05.810 --> 00:17:10.370
Was that a part of the equation
of these guys are different?

00:17:10.370 --> 00:17:12.476
No, that wasn't part of the equation.

00:17:12.476 --> 00:17:15.870
I do think you might have been zealous.

00:17:15.870 --> 00:17:18.688
But never really took that into account,

00:17:18.688 --> 00:17:19.583
to be honest with you.

00:17:19.583 --> 00:17:22.990
Because if you came from a place

00:17:22.990 --> 00:17:27.990
where you were doing things
that were off limits for us.

00:17:29.676 --> 00:17:32.640
So then once you came into the fold

00:17:32.640 --> 00:17:37.640
and Todd was trying to get
you into campus crusade.

00:17:38.760 --> 00:17:41.820
Once you became our
friend, it was just like,

00:17:41.820 --> 00:17:44.210
you were all in at that point.

00:17:44.210 --> 00:17:45.720
Which came first?

00:17:45.720 --> 00:17:47.630
Being friends with us or being Christians?

00:17:47.630 --> 00:17:52.630
Did we rope you into like
really going all in for Jesus?

00:17:53.250 --> 00:17:54.583
No, you guys didn't.

00:17:58.180 --> 00:18:01.790
Well this kind of goes to

00:18:01.790 --> 00:18:04.690
you guys' deconstruction.

00:18:04.690 --> 00:18:05.529
Yeah.

00:18:05.529 --> 00:18:08.990
I've thought about this a lot, actually,

00:18:08.990 --> 00:18:11.660
because when I became a Christian,

00:18:11.660 --> 00:18:13.563
I met you guys shortly thereafter.

00:18:14.841 --> 00:18:19.435
And I thought of like,

00:18:19.435 --> 00:18:21.160
what does this say about me?

00:18:21.160 --> 00:18:23.940
Like does their deconstruction,

00:18:23.940 --> 00:18:25.360
what effect does it have on me?

00:18:25.360 --> 00:18:29.460
But I kind of realized that.

00:18:29.460 --> 00:18:32.423
I deconstructed many years before.

00:18:33.540 --> 00:18:37.090
I grew up in a Christian home,
and at some point I said,

00:18:37.090 --> 00:18:38.163
this is not for me.

00:18:40.400 --> 00:18:42.290
I don't even know what I'm doing.

00:18:42.290 --> 00:18:45.410
So I kind of rethought the whole thing,

00:18:45.410 --> 00:18:46.890
and ended up where I am.

00:18:46.890 --> 00:18:49.480
Where I was at that point in time.

00:18:49.480 --> 00:18:52.970
And you guys were just great friends.

00:18:52.970 --> 00:18:54.530
I just loved being around you,

00:18:54.530 --> 00:18:59.300
and I still wish I could relive it all.

00:18:59.300 --> 00:19:02.733
It was a wonderful time
in my life to hang out

00:19:02.733 --> 00:19:05.713
and nothing beats those college years.

00:19:07.360 --> 00:19:10.320
Those are the best years that I remember.

00:19:10.320 --> 00:19:12.400
Yeah. Because it's like you said,

00:19:12.400 --> 00:19:14.820
the definition of entertainment

00:19:14.820 --> 00:19:18.450
was just the three of us
hanging out for hours,

00:19:18.450 --> 00:19:20.480
just talking, just talking.

00:19:20.480 --> 00:19:24.750
And I think there was no, yeah,
we were just getting a kick

00:19:24.750 --> 00:19:25.583
out of each other.

00:19:25.583 --> 00:19:27.180
It was just being on the same wavelength,

00:19:27.180 --> 00:19:29.980
and there just being
that, I also think, Gregg,

00:19:29.980 --> 00:19:33.000
you brought this dynamic
into our, I have to think,

00:19:33.000 --> 00:19:34.673
it's like, I'm projecting a little bit,

00:19:34.673 --> 00:19:37.620
because I don't remember
thinking this at the time,

00:19:37.620 --> 00:19:42.360
and I don't remember
us talking about Gregg,

00:19:42.360 --> 00:19:44.850
talking about you when you weren't around.

00:19:44.850 --> 00:19:47.050
But I have to think
looking back on it that,

00:19:48.170 --> 00:19:50.300
as close as Rhett and I were,

00:19:51.190 --> 00:19:55.410
there was thing to bring,
You mixed things up.

00:19:55.410 --> 00:19:59.623
Bringing a third person into the equation,

00:20:00.670 --> 00:20:02.330
it kind of opened things up.

00:20:02.330 --> 00:20:04.280
There's lots of things where it's like,

00:20:05.260 --> 00:20:08.670
if the two of us are talking,
and it's even true now,

00:20:08.670 --> 00:20:10.690
a lot of times, if we
hang out with people,

00:20:10.690 --> 00:20:13.150
there's this dynamic, that's like,

00:20:13.150 --> 00:20:15.263
we've heard so much about each other.

00:20:15.263 --> 00:20:17.600
We've lived so much of our lives together,

00:20:17.600 --> 00:20:18.470
and know so much.

00:20:18.470 --> 00:20:20.270
It's like you bring somebody into it,

00:20:20.270 --> 00:20:25.270
who's newer to the equation,
it opens up the conversation.

00:20:27.240 --> 00:20:29.120
It makes it where you can talk.

00:20:29.120 --> 00:20:30.340
You can cover new ground,

00:20:30.340 --> 00:20:32.250
you can have a different perspective.

00:20:32.250 --> 00:20:35.610
And I think you were
so, your sense of humor

00:20:35.610 --> 00:20:40.610
and just you were so
game for conversation,

00:20:40.730 --> 00:20:43.493
and you came from a different place.

00:20:44.600 --> 00:20:45.833
Well, Wilmington.

00:20:47.311 --> 00:20:50.427
But then it was just like,
this guy has a different vibe.

00:20:54.332 --> 00:20:56.170
He's cool. He's weird.

00:20:56.170 --> 00:20:58.523
He's got interesting
perspective. He's funny.

00:21:00.920 --> 00:21:03.300
I think it had to have breathed.

00:21:03.300 --> 00:21:06.070
It had been a breath of
fresh air into our friendship

00:21:06.070 --> 00:21:07.360
to open it up in that way.

00:21:07.360 --> 00:21:11.420
I kind of see, you guys
are, been best friends

00:21:11.420 --> 00:21:13.370
for a long time.

00:21:13.370 --> 00:21:16.850
And I think it speaks
volumes to your friendship.

00:21:16.850 --> 00:21:21.197
That you would allow a third
best friend into that dynamic,

00:21:21.197 --> 00:21:23.060
and it didn't affect you guys at all.

00:21:23.060 --> 00:21:25.320
I mean, I could hang out with Rhett

00:21:25.320 --> 00:21:26.310
or I could hang out with Link,

00:21:26.310 --> 00:21:27.470
or we could all hang out together,

00:21:27.470 --> 00:21:28.580
or y'all could and hang
out with each other,

00:21:28.580 --> 00:21:30.610
and it was like there was no question

00:21:31.510 --> 00:21:33.760
that the friendship was true.

00:21:33.760 --> 00:21:36.680
There was never any worrying

00:21:36.680 --> 00:21:39.570
that Rhett had a new best friend,

00:21:39.570 --> 00:21:41.133
or link had a new best friend.

00:21:42.718 --> 00:21:47.610
It was truly this perfect
three way friendship.

00:21:47.610 --> 00:21:49.090
It was a perfect three way.

00:21:49.090 --> 00:21:51.096
Yeah. That's how we
definitely thought of it.

00:21:51.096 --> 00:21:52.800
But yeah, I was thinking about that too.

00:21:52.800 --> 00:21:57.482
Well, I think that we were
always, without stating it,

00:21:57.482 --> 00:21:59.970
we were always looking for somebody

00:21:59.970 --> 00:22:02.563
to be like the third
person in our friendship,

00:22:03.630 --> 00:22:08.630
but that takes a special person, because--

00:22:08.750 --> 00:22:10.310
We didn't know we were looking for it,

00:22:10.310 --> 00:22:13.127
but I do think when we found it in Gregg,

00:22:13.127 --> 00:22:15.200
it was just very natural.

00:22:15.200 --> 00:22:18.450
It has it has happened a
few times in our lives,

00:22:18.450 --> 00:22:20.763
but the reason it's a
challenge is because,

00:22:22.150 --> 00:22:24.010
we both have to like the person,

00:22:24.010 --> 00:22:25.962
which isn't always the case. Right?

00:22:25.962 --> 00:22:26.795
Right.

00:22:26.795 --> 00:22:27.950
And it's not that you like
people that I don't like,

00:22:27.950 --> 00:22:29.280
or I like people that you don't like,

00:22:29.280 --> 00:22:32.240
it's that we are both pretty particular

00:22:32.240 --> 00:22:34.430
in terms of where we're
gonna invest our time

00:22:34.430 --> 00:22:36.191
and who's gonna be our friend,

00:22:36.191 --> 00:22:37.810
and that's kinda always been the case.

00:22:37.810 --> 00:22:40.240
We're both very tough, and as we said,

00:22:40.240 --> 00:22:45.050
we like spending time with
people, and then like retreating,

00:22:45.050 --> 00:22:48.620
and analyzing the person
and the experience,

00:22:48.620 --> 00:22:50.070
the two of us later.

00:22:50.070 --> 00:22:52.370
And I think if we ever
talked about you, Gregg,

00:22:52.370 --> 00:22:55.570
it was like all the stuff
that I've already said,

00:22:55.570 --> 00:22:58.890
and it was all positive,
and there was this vibe

00:22:58.890 --> 00:23:00.830
that, yeah, I'm actually very surprised

00:23:00.830 --> 00:23:04.640
that there was something about
the nature of our dynamic,

00:23:04.640 --> 00:23:07.700
that you brought something
that I wasn't threatened by.

00:23:07.700 --> 00:23:12.700
Because I have to think that
very well could have been part

00:23:13.300 --> 00:23:16.393
of the equation, but it never was.

00:23:16.393 --> 00:23:18.740
Because we did hang out the,

00:23:18.740 --> 00:23:21.820
I wanna talk about the dynamic
with how we did hang out,

00:23:21.820 --> 00:23:23.120
how we spent our time, it was based--

00:23:23.120 --> 00:23:24.200
You hung out with Gregg a lot more

00:23:24.200 --> 00:23:25.450
than I did during the school week.

00:23:25.450 --> 00:23:26.670
And we'll talk about why that was,

00:23:26.670 --> 00:23:29.900
but one of the things
that happened early on

00:23:29.900 --> 00:23:32.440
that sophomore year, I'll
was see if you remember this,

00:23:32.440 --> 00:23:34.953
and I don't remember
exactly how it came up,

00:23:35.900 --> 00:23:37.500
but we got you to cut your hair.

00:23:39.210 --> 00:23:41.950
If you remember your-

00:23:41.950 --> 00:23:42.783
Mop top.

00:23:42.783 --> 00:23:44.420
Your freshman year haircut,

00:23:44.420 --> 00:23:47.420
and then you went, basically, just buzz,

00:23:47.420 --> 00:23:49.930
and you basically had the buzz cut

00:23:49.930 --> 00:23:52.850
for a very long time after that.

00:23:52.850 --> 00:23:53.683
Do you remember what--

00:23:53.683 --> 00:23:55.185
Did I cut his hair?

00:23:55.185 --> 00:23:57.080
Did I cut your hair?
Probably.

00:23:57.080 --> 00:23:58.670
I'm sure we all cut each other's hair.

00:23:58.670 --> 00:24:00.130
We had the Clippers.

00:24:00.130 --> 00:24:01.630
Yeah.

00:24:01.630 --> 00:24:04.240
You were cutting my hair
in college, for sure.

00:24:04.240 --> 00:24:07.690
You don't remember us talking
you into cutting your hair?

00:24:07.690 --> 00:24:10.870
No, I thought I always had that haircut.

00:24:10.870 --> 00:24:12.630
No dude. When we met you--

00:24:12.630 --> 00:24:15.770
Freshman year, man, you had
a lot of hair on top of head.

00:24:15.770 --> 00:24:17.460
Looked like a wig.

00:24:17.460 --> 00:24:19.810
Well, we called it the mushroom.

00:24:19.810 --> 00:24:20.870
You had a mushroom, dude.

00:24:20.870 --> 00:24:23.470
We gave you a makeover and then you guys,

00:24:23.470 --> 00:24:26.093
I mean, whenever you guys
would hang out without me,

00:24:26.093 --> 00:24:27.387
you'd be playing Twisted Metal.

00:24:27.387 --> 00:24:29.283
Well, but let's establish why.

00:24:30.590 --> 00:24:33.740
You studied all the time.

00:24:33.740 --> 00:24:36.150
I studied a lot, in my freshman year.

00:24:36.150 --> 00:24:38.790
I studied a whole lot in my freshman year.

00:24:38.790 --> 00:24:42.020
I got all A's and I was like, okay.

00:24:42.020 --> 00:24:43.220
Been there, done that.

00:24:45.960 --> 00:24:47.720
This doesn't need to be
what college is about.

00:24:47.720 --> 00:24:49.730
So starting sophomore year, I was like,

00:24:49.730 --> 00:24:51.960
I'm gonna study enough to get outta here

00:24:51.960 --> 00:24:54.720
with a respectable GPA but--

00:24:54.720 --> 00:24:57.930
But you also had another
guy, who wasn't me

00:24:57.930 --> 00:25:00.590
that gave you opportunity to do stuff.

00:25:00.590 --> 00:25:01.423
Well, and then Gregg.

00:25:01.423 --> 00:25:02.690
When I was not giving you any opportunity.

00:25:02.690 --> 00:25:04.140
Gregg is famous for the line,

00:25:04.140 --> 00:25:06.320
when I was over there at his place,

00:25:06.320 --> 00:25:07.890
and we were getting ready
to play another round--

00:25:07.890 --> 00:25:08.740
See if he remembers it?

00:25:08.740 --> 00:25:10.190
of twisted metal.

00:25:10.190 --> 00:25:11.840
'Cause that was our
game. You remember that?

00:25:11.840 --> 00:25:14.020
Twisted metal was our game.

00:25:14.020 --> 00:25:16.810
Now, do you remember what
you told me when I was like,

00:25:16.810 --> 00:25:18.150
I gotta go study, man.

00:25:18.150 --> 00:25:19.888
You know what you know,
you don't learn anything.

00:25:21.396 --> 00:25:22.291
I live by that.

00:25:22.291 --> 00:25:23.817
You know what you know,
you don't learn anything.

00:25:23.817 --> 00:25:26.310
And then there was like
this corollary of like

00:25:26.310 --> 00:25:30.400
everything I know I
learned in kindergarten.

00:25:30.400 --> 00:25:31.610
Was that also you?

00:25:31.610 --> 00:25:33.380
No, I don't think so.

00:25:33.380 --> 00:25:34.213
Okay.

00:25:35.770 --> 00:25:37.130
The catch phrase.

00:25:37.130 --> 00:25:38.069
I mean, me and Gregg--

00:25:38.069 --> 00:25:39.549
You know what you know
you don't learn anything.

00:25:39.549 --> 00:25:41.550
It's interesting in the dynamic
that you're talking about

00:25:41.550 --> 00:25:43.790
with not feeling threatened
because Gregg and I

00:25:43.790 --> 00:25:45.950
did end up spending a lot of time together

00:25:45.950 --> 00:25:48.853
playing video games,
eating Gumbee's eat pizza.

00:25:51.600 --> 00:25:52.870
You've never really been into video games.

00:25:52.870 --> 00:25:54.620
You're not really into food.

00:25:54.620 --> 00:25:58.180
So the idea of we're gonna go
and get off extra large pizza

00:25:58.180 --> 00:25:59.777
and sit on a bench and
eat the whole thing.

00:25:59.777 --> 00:26:01.720
That's just not Link Neal stuff.

00:26:01.720 --> 00:26:02.553
So it was like, yeah.

00:26:02.553 --> 00:26:03.810
But I had somebody who was like,

00:26:03.810 --> 00:26:06.220
yeah, I do like to eat pizza
in the middle of the night.

00:26:06.220 --> 00:26:08.397
And I do like to not
study and play video game.

00:26:08.397 --> 00:26:10.700
And so me and Gregg had something.

00:26:10.700 --> 00:26:13.220
So for you, from your perspective,

00:26:13.220 --> 00:26:17.230
Gregg filled a felt need that you had

00:26:17.230 --> 00:26:20.400
that I couldn't fulfill
as your best friend.

00:26:20.400 --> 00:26:22.530
I wonder if we look at it the other way.

00:26:22.530 --> 00:26:27.420
For me, what did we do
together, Gregg, without Rhett?

00:26:31.120 --> 00:26:31.953
I don't know.

00:26:31.953 --> 00:26:36.440
I know that the years after college,

00:26:36.440 --> 00:26:37.690
I hung out with you more,

00:26:37.690 --> 00:26:39.820
a lot more than I hung out with Rhett.

00:26:39.820 --> 00:26:42.530
It's like almost two
different stages there.

00:26:42.530 --> 00:26:43.363
Yeah.
Yeah.

00:26:43.363 --> 00:26:46.630
We had a post-college stage

00:26:46.630 --> 00:26:51.630
where we were both newly
weds, starting our marriages,

00:26:53.790 --> 00:26:55.640
Christie and Jim were good friends.

00:26:55.640 --> 00:26:56.510
But you go to the same church.

00:26:56.510 --> 00:26:57.800
We went to the same church.

00:26:57.800 --> 00:26:58.633
Ah, yeah.

00:26:58.633 --> 00:27:02.810
So that's why we had now first stage

00:27:02.810 --> 00:27:05.840
of post-college adulthood,

00:27:05.840 --> 00:27:07.110
I would go over to your house,

00:27:07.110 --> 00:27:10.130
and you still had that
Homer Simpson grill.

00:27:10.130 --> 00:27:12.760
It was a yellow grill that
looked like from The Simpsons.

00:27:12.760 --> 00:27:13.647
I have one of Gregg's bristle--

00:27:13.647 --> 00:27:14.595
Still have it.

00:27:14.595 --> 00:27:15.504
You till have it?

00:27:15.504 --> 00:27:16.370
Still have it. Yeah.

00:27:16.370 --> 00:27:18.260
But to go back to sophomore year.

00:27:18.260 --> 00:27:19.590
Yeah. So you would go over there,

00:27:19.590 --> 00:27:20.840
you would play twisted metal,

00:27:20.840 --> 00:27:24.170
and then let's get into
the prime time of it all,

00:27:24.170 --> 00:27:29.031
because you took over,
Garrett was a senior,

00:27:29.031 --> 00:27:30.690
a freshman year-ish.

00:27:30.690 --> 00:27:33.860
He was the MC, he was leaving.

00:27:33.860 --> 00:27:36.780
He pulled you under his wing,
and you were just waiting

00:27:36.780 --> 00:27:41.770
for him and Todd to ask you
to step up and be the MC.

00:27:41.770 --> 00:27:43.250
You were lobbying for that.

00:27:43.250 --> 00:27:44.327
Yeah.

00:27:44.327 --> 00:27:46.640
And I was in support of that,

00:27:46.640 --> 00:27:51.610
but it was like there was
these recruiting conversations.

00:27:51.610 --> 00:27:54.710
It was interesting. But
then you got the job.

00:27:54.710 --> 00:27:56.270
It was no pay.

00:27:56.270 --> 00:28:00.350
And then, because I was already upfront.

00:28:00.350 --> 00:28:04.710
We were both in that the most
visible roles as students

00:28:04.710 --> 00:28:08.580
to shape what the weekly
meeting looked like,

00:28:08.580 --> 00:28:13.043
and, of course we would talk about,

00:28:15.600 --> 00:28:16.730
you'd be working up stuff

00:28:16.730 --> 00:28:18.560
that you were gonna do in your monologue.

00:28:18.560 --> 00:28:20.470
I'd like to walk through
what a primetime episode

00:28:20.470 --> 00:28:23.250
would look like, but of course, Gregg,

00:28:23.250 --> 00:28:27.240
you were wrapped up in all
this because we were a trio.

00:28:27.240 --> 00:28:31.020
If we were both shaping the
meeting and having ideas,

00:28:31.020 --> 00:28:36.020
I think pretty quickly, you
got involved on stage two,

00:28:37.200 --> 00:28:42.200
basically, as the Andy
Richter to Rhett's Conan

00:28:43.160 --> 00:28:46.030
or Ed McMahon to his Johnny Carson.

00:28:46.030 --> 00:28:47.904
Well, I just remember him thinking that--

00:28:47.904 --> 00:28:50.140
Like a sidekick.

00:28:50.140 --> 00:28:51.150
Gregg was hilarious,

00:28:51.150 --> 00:28:53.280
that everybody thought that he was funny,

00:28:53.280 --> 00:28:57.260
and he had really interesting
ways of saying things.

00:28:57.260 --> 00:28:58.093
Yeah.

00:28:58.093 --> 00:29:00.280
And so I was just like,
why don't you come up

00:29:00.280 --> 00:29:01.730
and just give a deep thought?

00:29:01.730 --> 00:29:05.020
So it was obviously a throwback
to the Jack Handey thing

00:29:05.020 --> 00:29:07.610
from the SNL days, but it was just like,

00:29:07.610 --> 00:29:09.100
Gregg, do you have a deep thought for us?

00:29:09.100 --> 00:29:13.657
And then I honestly don't
remember how those were concocted.

00:29:15.660 --> 00:29:16.493
I don't know.

00:29:16.493 --> 00:29:18.140
I remember how they were concocted.

00:29:18.140 --> 00:29:18.973
Okay.

00:29:18.973 --> 00:29:21.170
We would sit back before the meeting,

00:29:21.170 --> 00:29:23.777
and We would all come up
with this deep thought,

00:29:23.777 --> 00:29:27.370
and you guys would perfectly
plan out what I was gonna say.

00:29:27.370 --> 00:29:29.173
And I could never say it.

00:29:31.010 --> 00:29:32.660
Never say it quite as written-

00:29:32.660 --> 00:29:33.493
But there was a--

00:29:33.493 --> 00:29:36.150
Which was part of the
thing that made it great.

00:29:36.150 --> 00:29:37.793
And then a lot of times,

00:29:39.849 --> 00:29:42.053
You and I would be working
on it because, Rhett,

00:29:42.053 --> 00:29:45.910
you'd be working on your monologue.

00:29:45.910 --> 00:29:46.743
Right.

00:29:46.743 --> 00:29:49.130
So then while you're
getting prepared last minute

00:29:49.130 --> 00:29:52.120
for that, Gregg, you and I
would be over on the side,

00:29:52.120 --> 00:29:55.800
and I would be drilling
you in your deep thought.

00:29:55.800 --> 00:29:59.230
I mean, you do remember
the preamble to it, right?

00:29:59.230 --> 00:30:00.360
That was the same every time.

00:30:00.360 --> 00:30:01.300
Yeah. Yeah.

00:30:01.300 --> 00:30:03.370
I didn't mess that up. I don't think.

00:30:03.370 --> 00:30:04.203
Let's hear it.

00:30:04.203 --> 00:30:06.960
After much deep thought
and great meditation,

00:30:06.960 --> 00:30:09.190
I've come to the realization.

00:30:09.190 --> 00:30:10.440
That.

00:30:10.440 --> 00:30:11.273
That...

00:30:11.273 --> 00:30:12.955
And then you would say it.

00:30:12.955 --> 00:30:15.390
I don't know. I don't remember one.

00:30:15.390 --> 00:30:17.244
Yeah. I don't remember any of that.

00:30:17.244 --> 00:30:19.370
After much deep thought
and great meditation,

00:30:19.370 --> 00:30:20.960
I've come to the realization

00:30:20.960 --> 00:30:23.973
that if you're gonna walk backwards,

00:30:25.100 --> 00:30:28.310
you're only seeing where you've been,

00:30:28.310 --> 00:30:31.280
but that's not a bad thing,

00:30:31.280 --> 00:30:33.620
if you're about to walk off a cliff.

00:30:33.620 --> 00:30:35.160
I don't know. I made that up,

00:30:35.160 --> 00:30:38.530
but they would be that non sequitur--

00:30:38.530 --> 00:30:39.610
And they were very unrelated.

00:30:39.610 --> 00:30:42.260
They weren't themed to anything
that I was talking about,

00:30:42.260 --> 00:30:43.850
because what I was doing every single week

00:30:43.850 --> 00:30:48.850
is, I mean, this is one of
my favorite times in my life,

00:30:50.520 --> 00:30:53.460
because I had this audience,

00:30:53.460 --> 00:30:57.380
and I basically was like, I'm
writing a standup routine,

00:30:57.380 --> 00:31:00.050
an original standup
routine every single week.

00:31:00.050 --> 00:31:03.650
I was much more excited about
that than I was studying.

00:31:03.650 --> 00:31:04.483
And it was just like,

00:31:04.483 --> 00:31:06.450
okay, well, this week,
I'm gonna tell this story,

00:31:06.450 --> 00:31:07.940
or this week, I'm gonna do this.

00:31:07.940 --> 00:31:09.310
And I remember one week I was like,

00:31:09.310 --> 00:31:12.880
I had gotten so much confidence.
Was way overconfident.

00:31:12.880 --> 00:31:15.940
I was like this week, I'm not
letting myself think about

00:31:15.940 --> 00:31:17.320
any of it until I'm up there,

00:31:17.320 --> 00:31:18.860
and I'm doing it all off the cuff.

00:31:18.860 --> 00:31:20.733
I don't think that one went that well.

00:31:27.397 --> 00:31:29.770
And of course there was the video aspect,

00:31:29.770 --> 00:31:31.480
probably was about one out of 10.

00:31:31.480 --> 00:31:34.600
Let's paint a picture
for how this went, so--

00:31:34.600 --> 00:31:36.110
We did that two times a semester.

00:31:36.110 --> 00:31:39.640
The weekly meeting every Thursday night,

00:31:39.640 --> 00:31:42.776
it would start I think when you started,

00:31:42.776 --> 00:31:45.030
maybe it might have been
a few hundred people,

00:31:45.030 --> 00:31:48.110
and then that was sophomore
year, junior year, senior year,

00:31:48.110 --> 00:31:53.110
by senior year, it was
the largest campus group.

00:31:53.210 --> 00:31:55.100
There was like over a
thousand students coming

00:31:55.100 --> 00:31:55.933
to this thing.

00:31:55.933 --> 00:31:58.640
They had to start moving the venue.

00:31:58.640 --> 00:32:03.610
And it wasn't just
because of any one thing.

00:32:03.610 --> 00:32:07.370
I mean, the fact that, yeah,

00:32:07.370 --> 00:32:10.040
there was a lot of comedy
involved, it was very fun.

00:32:10.040 --> 00:32:13.270
It was a fun environment to
be in, so that was a factor.

00:32:13.270 --> 00:32:15.500
There were other factors too,
but I think it definitely

00:32:15.500 --> 00:32:16.960
was a positive contribution

00:32:16.960 --> 00:32:19.470
to the numbers growing every week.

00:32:19.470 --> 00:32:20.950
It became kind of this spectacle,

00:32:20.950 --> 00:32:22.810
if you're gonna show up to this thing.

00:32:22.810 --> 00:32:27.810
And in my mind, I was
very much a part of it.

00:32:29.530 --> 00:32:30.750
Obviously in your mind,

00:32:30.750 --> 00:32:33.160
you were the center of it as the host,

00:32:33.160 --> 00:32:38.160
and Gregg is sidekick, but
Whenever we'd get together,

00:32:38.420 --> 00:32:40.560
sophomore year, and then junior year,

00:32:40.560 --> 00:32:41.950
when we moved into an apartment,

00:32:41.950 --> 00:32:43.540
the three of us together,

00:32:43.540 --> 00:32:46.510
it was like the epicenter of planning,

00:32:46.510 --> 00:32:48.280
working with Todd on planning an episode,

00:32:48.280 --> 00:32:50.700
I mean, a meeting.

00:32:50.700 --> 00:32:53.640
So the meeting would start with singing,

00:32:53.640 --> 00:32:55.220
and I would lead the singing.

00:32:55.220 --> 00:32:57.470
So I would be up there
and like saying stuff

00:32:57.470 --> 00:32:59.500
in between songs and kind of scratching

00:32:59.500 --> 00:33:02.173
that itch of like having an audience.

00:33:03.350 --> 00:33:04.183
But it was very spiritual.

00:33:04.183 --> 00:33:07.530
It was very spiritual. I
had to stay in my lane.

00:33:07.530 --> 00:33:10.270
But then the next thing that would happen

00:33:10.270 --> 00:33:15.270
is you would come out as
the MC and do a welcome,

00:33:15.500 --> 00:33:18.910
but a lot of times we saw
that as an opportunity

00:33:18.910 --> 00:33:20.671
to create a video,

00:33:20.671 --> 00:33:22.680
and they had done this in years past,

00:33:22.680 --> 00:33:27.680
create a video where it would
be this comedy lead up to you.

00:33:29.350 --> 00:33:31.290
It would always be you're doing something,

00:33:31.290 --> 00:33:33.570
some sort of adventure or something,

00:33:33.570 --> 00:33:35.512
and then the way the
video would always end is

00:33:35.512 --> 00:33:38.250
you walking up the stairs
to the front of the venue,

00:33:38.250 --> 00:33:40.440
and then you would come
in and you'd be there.

00:33:40.440 --> 00:33:44.330
So it would seem like a
continuation of the video live.

00:33:44.330 --> 00:33:46.610
And in reality, I think
we probably only did that

00:33:46.610 --> 00:33:48.260
at the beginning of each semester

00:33:49.690 --> 00:33:51.650
Because, literally, we
had to make the videos,

00:33:51.650 --> 00:33:55.240
and then I was going home to
my dad's office in Buoys Creek

00:33:55.240 --> 00:33:56.801
and editing them on two VCRs.

00:33:56.801 --> 00:34:00.823
This was still before a
non-linear editing system.

00:34:02.267 --> 00:34:04.810
If we were in college right now,

00:34:04.810 --> 00:34:06.390
we would've been doing
videos every single week,

00:34:06.390 --> 00:34:09.180
but we didn't have the capacity to do it.

00:34:09.180 --> 00:34:11.530
So at first you would just come out,

00:34:11.530 --> 00:34:12.720
maybe without a video,

00:34:12.720 --> 00:34:14.920
but then when we started making videos,

00:34:14.920 --> 00:34:18.070
you and Gregg would come out as a duo.

00:34:18.070 --> 00:34:18.903
Yeah, yeah, yeah.

00:34:18.903 --> 00:34:19.920
As a comedy duo.

00:34:19.920 --> 00:34:21.160
The two of you would come out,

00:34:21.160 --> 00:34:23.350
and so the video was a set piece

00:34:23.350 --> 00:34:26.500
to introduce the two of you,
whenever we could make those.

00:34:26.500 --> 00:34:31.500
And I was the director,
DP, I was the cameraman.

00:34:32.470 --> 00:34:33.303
Right.

00:34:33.303 --> 00:34:38.050
And you and I were very
involved in coming up

00:34:38.050 --> 00:34:39.970
with what these videos would be,

00:34:39.970 --> 00:34:44.970
and then it would be kind
of some sort of storyline,

00:34:45.660 --> 00:34:47.930
sometimes Gregg would be lost

00:34:47.930 --> 00:34:50.040
or you would be fighting. I don't know.

00:34:50.040 --> 00:34:52.620
I had to find Gregg a couple
of times, and then I think--

00:34:52.620 --> 00:34:54.380
In one of them, you actually carried--

00:34:54.380 --> 00:34:55.213
I carried you in.

00:34:55.213 --> 00:34:59.130
You carried Gregg in,
like he was passed out

00:34:59.130 --> 00:35:00.280
or dead or something.

00:35:00.280 --> 00:35:03.563
I never thought of this, but
why did I never find Rhett?

00:35:05.400 --> 00:35:07.866
You were the sidekick.
You were the side kick.

00:35:07.866 --> 00:35:10.680
I had to find you in
order to feel complete.

00:35:10.680 --> 00:35:13.030
You would come down the aisle together

00:35:13.030 --> 00:35:15.390
and then Rhett would be,
you would stand on the side,

00:35:15.390 --> 00:35:17.120
Rhett would do his monologue,

00:35:17.120 --> 00:35:20.390
I would be sitting in the
audience, laughing the loudest.

00:35:20.390 --> 00:35:22.110
I was basically doing what Stevie does

00:35:22.110 --> 00:35:23.080
in "Good Mythical Morning."

00:35:23.080 --> 00:35:26.357
You would let the crowd, the
person who sits under the-

00:35:26.357 --> 00:35:28.630
I'd break the icebreaker. I'd break--

00:35:28.630 --> 00:35:29.680
Everybody know it's okay to laugh.

00:35:29.680 --> 00:35:30.860
It's okay to laugh.

00:35:30.860 --> 00:35:31.840
Applause coach.

00:35:31.840 --> 00:35:32.950
Yeah.

00:35:32.950 --> 00:35:35.010
I would laugh, not applaud.

00:35:35.010 --> 00:35:37.170
So Gregg, you would be standing
up there the whole time

00:35:37.170 --> 00:35:38.590
that Rhett was giving his monologue,

00:35:38.590 --> 00:35:41.320
and occasionally, Rhett would turn to you,

00:35:41.320 --> 00:35:44.910
and have an exchange if he wanted to.

00:35:44.910 --> 00:35:47.990
But otherwise you were
just kind of standing there

00:35:47.990 --> 00:35:51.270
awkwardly, waiting to
give your deep thought,

00:35:51.270 --> 00:35:54.446
which was hilarious, not the deep thought.

00:35:54.446 --> 00:35:56.800
The fact that you were
just standing up there

00:35:56.800 --> 00:35:58.303
was just kind of funny to me.

00:36:00.150 --> 00:36:03.690
And then you would say
something like Gregg,

00:36:03.690 --> 00:36:05.710
and you would know it
would have a pattern,

00:36:05.710 --> 00:36:09.010
video or not monologue.

00:36:09.010 --> 00:36:11.697
And then you would turn to Gregg and say,

00:36:11.697 --> 00:36:14.860
"Hey, Gregg," or you might
say, "What's up. Gregg?"

00:36:14.860 --> 00:36:16.241
and he'd say, "Good."

00:36:16.241 --> 00:36:17.820
And I'd say, "Gregg, you've
been thinking about anything?"

00:36:17.820 --> 00:36:19.240
We always set it up as--

00:36:19.240 --> 00:36:20.073
So everyone would, expect--

00:36:20.073 --> 00:36:21.163
It was a natural conversation.

00:36:21.163 --> 00:36:24.150
this really anti-climatic deep thought

00:36:24.150 --> 00:36:28.890
that I was waiting for
you to totally screw up,

00:36:28.890 --> 00:36:31.330
which was an added layer of,

00:36:31.330 --> 00:36:33.300
but you were good at it.

00:36:33.300 --> 00:36:34.750
Something about you delivery.

00:36:34.750 --> 00:36:36.960
People, loved it. People
loved it every time.

00:36:36.960 --> 00:36:40.350
And then after that, we'd
probably sing some more songs,

00:36:40.350 --> 00:36:42.860
there might be promotions
for different events,

00:36:42.860 --> 00:36:44.030
and then there'd be a speaker,

00:36:44.030 --> 00:36:45.730
and then there'd be more
songs, and that would be it.

00:36:45.730 --> 00:36:50.090
So it's like really the
beginning was about the comedy.

00:36:50.090 --> 00:36:51.490
Well, that's the interesting thing

00:36:51.490 --> 00:36:54.210
is that the beginning of every meeting,

00:36:54.210 --> 00:36:57.343
in addition to the funny
monologue, I would have to say,

00:36:58.617 --> 00:36:59.667
"Campus Crusade for Crisis

00:36:59.667 --> 00:37:01.257
"Internominational Christian Organization

00:37:01.257 --> 00:37:02.573
"founded in 1951 by."

00:37:02.573 --> 00:37:05.490
And I would do the spiel,
and I would be like

00:37:05.490 --> 00:37:08.590
we operate on a three, I would
make it different every time,

00:37:08.590 --> 00:37:11.800
but say three pronged
philosophy of win, build, send.

00:37:11.800 --> 00:37:13.890
Win students to Christ,
build them up in their faith,

00:37:13.890 --> 00:37:16.290
and send them out to continue
the win, build send process.

00:37:16.290 --> 00:37:18.483
I said, stuff like that all the time.

00:37:19.340 --> 00:37:20.760
You became because spokesman.

00:37:20.760 --> 00:37:24.950
And then there was this weird dynamic of,

00:37:24.950 --> 00:37:27.390
and this really became a
thing during Christmas carols

00:37:27.390 --> 00:37:30.380
but trying to be as funny as possible,

00:37:30.380 --> 00:37:31.920
and then being spiritual,

00:37:31.920 --> 00:37:34.410
because a pastor would come up and speak

00:37:34.410 --> 00:37:36.878
and do a serious talk.

00:37:36.878 --> 00:37:39.280
It usually started humorously,

00:37:39.280 --> 00:37:41.360
and then ended in a serious place.

00:37:41.360 --> 00:37:42.810
And then I gotta get up there after that.

00:37:42.810 --> 00:37:46.050
And at this point, all
the funny games are over,

00:37:46.050 --> 00:37:47.030
and I gotta say something

00:37:47.030 --> 00:37:50.880
that sounds like the student
response to this thing.

00:37:50.880 --> 00:37:51.750
It was such a weird dynamic--

00:37:51.750 --> 00:37:53.900
That talk really had an impact on you.

00:37:53.900 --> 00:37:55.470
But Gregg, the thing I
wanna talk to you about

00:37:55.470 --> 00:38:00.470
is do you know that, so
obviously, after we graduated

00:38:00.540 --> 00:38:01.690
and we were engineers for a while

00:38:01.690 --> 00:38:04.320
then Link and I went on staff for crusade

00:38:04.320 --> 00:38:08.240
to kind of do our comedy ministry thing.

00:38:08.240 --> 00:38:11.260
And during the support raising process,

00:38:11.260 --> 00:38:13.560
you would have to kind of talk about,

00:38:13.560 --> 00:38:14.720
you gotta give your spiel

00:38:14.720 --> 00:38:16.640
and kind of explain what you're doing.

00:38:16.640 --> 00:38:18.653
And Link was Link--

00:38:18.653 --> 00:38:20.730
I was gonna bring this
up because I was there

00:38:20.730 --> 00:38:22.113
for one of these meetings.

00:38:22.113 --> 00:38:24.010
Oh. You were?

00:38:24.010 --> 00:38:27.470
Yes, I was there when Link was presenting

00:38:27.470 --> 00:38:31.610
the fact that Rhett and
Link were were hosting

00:38:31.610 --> 00:38:34.180
the weekly meeting, and I looked at Link,

00:38:34.180 --> 00:38:35.175
and I was like mm.

00:38:37.130 --> 00:38:39.310
Yeah. And Link's simplified story

00:38:39.310 --> 00:38:40.860
that he would give to supporters,

00:38:40.860 --> 00:38:44.369
he overwrote you with himself.

00:38:45.737 --> 00:38:48.030
And you were in the room for it.

00:38:48.030 --> 00:38:49.550
Yeah. I heard the spill.

00:38:49.550 --> 00:38:51.580
I was there for one of the presentations.

00:38:51.580 --> 00:38:53.230
I think you gave me some money.

00:38:54.679 --> 00:38:56.060
Thank you.

00:38:56.060 --> 00:38:56.893
Well, listen.

00:38:58.070 --> 00:38:59.760
To me, I was justified in doing it

00:38:59.760 --> 00:39:01.861
because it was just too
complicated to try to explain.

00:39:01.861 --> 00:39:04.550
I completely understand why
you did it. You weren't--

00:39:04.550 --> 00:39:05.490
Were you upset?

00:39:05.490 --> 00:39:06.960
No.

00:39:06.960 --> 00:39:09.130
Yeah. You were talking to
like a 65 year old person

00:39:09.130 --> 00:39:10.360
in Kinston North Carolina.

00:39:10.360 --> 00:39:11.193
Yeah. Who?

00:39:11.193 --> 00:39:12.929
Well, apparently I was
talking to Gregg at one time.

00:39:15.110 --> 00:39:17.220
For some reason you and
I went up to Asheville

00:39:17.220 --> 00:39:21.220
for something and you were
scrounging for supporters,

00:39:21.220 --> 00:39:23.970
and I was like, well, I met
some people in Asheville,

00:39:23.970 --> 00:39:26.110
so these were my contacts.

00:39:26.110 --> 00:39:27.721
Yes.

00:39:27.721 --> 00:39:29.260
So you took to him to Asheville,

00:39:29.260 --> 00:39:30.657
and then he told the
story about me and him.

00:39:30.657 --> 00:39:31.650
That's him.

00:39:31.650 --> 00:39:32.996
Yeah.

00:39:32.996 --> 00:39:35.040
And it probably didn't occur
to me as I was saying it.

00:39:35.040 --> 00:39:36.130
I don't think they supported you,

00:39:36.130 --> 00:39:37.270
they could see through your like...

00:39:38.127 --> 00:39:39.953
Yeah. Yeah. I'm glad.

00:39:39.953 --> 00:39:41.883
You had the perfect
opportunity to be like,

00:39:41.883 --> 00:39:43.643
when Gregg was up there.

00:39:45.680 --> 00:39:47.590
You could have used Gregg's
connection to these people.

00:39:47.590 --> 00:39:48.560
You probably come outta there--

00:39:48.560 --> 00:39:51.400
I was using Gregg. That's
very clear at this point,

00:39:51.400 --> 00:39:53.350
but not in the right way.

00:39:53.350 --> 00:39:58.350
Gregg, I'm sorry for erasing
you in my revisionos history.

00:39:58.360 --> 00:40:01.120
Before that, I think there were moments

00:40:01.120 --> 00:40:05.300
when you moved from, by junior year,

00:40:05.300 --> 00:40:08.760
they asked you, Rhett to be the MC

00:40:08.760 --> 00:40:11.100
for the regional Christmas conference,

00:40:11.100 --> 00:40:14.133
and by this point, when you did that,

00:40:16.610 --> 00:40:19.310
I was involved less at first,

00:40:19.310 --> 00:40:20.820
but then more and more over time,

00:40:20.820 --> 00:40:24.600
and that's where we became
a comedy duo on stage.

00:40:24.600 --> 00:40:25.433
Yeah.

00:40:25.433 --> 00:40:26.560
But the way that that happened was,

00:40:26.560 --> 00:40:29.420
first of all, Gregg, I'm
interested in your perspective

00:40:29.420 --> 00:40:32.623
on this because at that point,

00:40:35.120 --> 00:40:38.740
the Gregg sidekick thing
didn't, I don't remember how,

00:40:38.740 --> 00:40:40.300
but it didn't transfer over

00:40:40.300 --> 00:40:42.327
to the regional Christmas conference.

00:40:42.327 --> 00:40:43.830
And I didn't replace you--

00:40:43.830 --> 00:40:44.663
I remember this.

00:40:44.663 --> 00:40:45.496
immediately.

00:40:46.506 --> 00:40:48.773
Okay. So the first year I did it,

00:40:50.180 --> 00:40:53.690
the only time that you were
on Sage was during the music.

00:40:53.690 --> 00:40:54.523
Right.

00:40:54.523 --> 00:40:56.690
Was during the comedy music,
not the worship music,

00:40:56.690 --> 00:40:57.980
the original songs.

00:40:57.980 --> 00:40:59.304
That we would write together.

00:40:59.304 --> 00:41:02.280
And the first first song
was the Unibrown song,

00:41:02.280 --> 00:41:05.530
which was the song that we
wrote when we wrote the song

00:41:05.530 --> 00:41:09.137
for your rehearsal dinner, which was,

00:41:09.137 --> 00:41:10.870
"We've seen Gregg naked,
soon you will too,"

00:41:10.870 --> 00:41:11.960
whatever that one.

00:41:11.960 --> 00:41:13.180
Hope you enjoy it more than we do.

00:41:13.180 --> 00:41:17.100
So when we got up there,
it was actually you

00:41:17.100 --> 00:41:18.860
and Tim and Gregg.

00:41:18.860 --> 00:41:19.777
Nope. Gregg wasn't there.

00:41:19.777 --> 00:41:21.520
It was just you and Tim?

00:41:21.520 --> 00:41:22.780
No. And I think the reason--

00:41:22.780 --> 00:41:23.613
I was getting married.

00:41:23.613 --> 00:41:24.446
I was getting married right then.

00:41:24.446 --> 00:41:25.440
You were moving on.

00:41:25.440 --> 00:41:26.930
That's why you went out of the picture.

00:41:26.930 --> 00:41:29.520
You didn't go to Christmas conference,

00:41:29.520 --> 00:41:31.666
you were engaged, you were moving on.

00:41:31.666 --> 00:41:36.666
We had written and
performed at least one song

00:41:37.070 --> 00:41:41.890
for seniors at the NC
State weekly meeting.

00:41:41.890 --> 00:41:44.490
So we had started writing some songs.

00:41:44.490 --> 00:41:45.323
Yeah.

00:41:45.323 --> 00:41:47.290
That first song that we ever performed

00:41:47.290 --> 00:41:50.650
at a Prime Time NC State
meeting for seniors,

00:41:50.650 --> 00:41:53.120
I don't wanna go into the details of it.

00:41:53.120 --> 00:41:56.420
But it was me.

00:41:56.420 --> 00:42:00.970
By senior year, we had Tim move
in with us in our apartment.

00:42:00.970 --> 00:42:04.900
So me, you and Tim were each playing

00:42:04.900 --> 00:42:08.440
a three-headed person who
was singing back up to you-

00:42:08.440 --> 00:42:09.273
Yeah.

00:42:09.273 --> 00:42:11.140
Singing, so that was the
first song we ever played.

00:42:11.140 --> 00:42:12.976
But then the second song that I remember

00:42:12.976 --> 00:42:15.860
was the Unibrown song,

00:42:15.860 --> 00:42:17.427
which Tim still came up there for that.

00:42:17.427 --> 00:42:18.300
And Christmas carols.

00:42:18.300 --> 00:42:19.133
And then after that,

00:42:19.133 --> 00:42:23.770
there was no more Tim playing,
singing songs with us,

00:42:23.770 --> 00:42:27.510
and I moved more into a sidekick
and then a co-host role.

00:42:27.510 --> 00:42:30.020
Because once I was no longer a student,

00:42:30.020 --> 00:42:32.800
and me and you were doing
our comedy thing together,

00:42:32.800 --> 00:42:34.814
and also talking a about going on staff

00:42:34.814 --> 00:42:38.550
is when, right at the beginning
of the whole conference,

00:42:38.550 --> 00:42:39.940
we would both get up there together.

00:42:39.940 --> 00:42:42.505
So that was the third year
or something like that.

00:42:42.505 --> 00:42:45.840
So in retrospect, from that point,

00:42:45.840 --> 00:42:48.770
Rhett and I were a comedy duo,
we were doing this full time.

00:42:48.770 --> 00:42:50.548
And when you worked
backwards, we had done it

00:42:50.548 --> 00:42:54.210
in Christmas Conference
so many years that,

00:42:54.210 --> 00:42:58.090
when you go back four or five years,

00:42:58.090 --> 00:43:00.390
then yeah, I kind of
become you in the story

00:43:00.390 --> 00:43:02.813
just so I can get that money.

00:43:03.670 --> 00:43:04.740
Now just to simplify.

00:43:04.740 --> 00:43:06.680
Well, Gregg, did you ever
find yourself thinking,

00:43:06.680 --> 00:43:09.710
man, I mean, he's not asking me

00:43:09.710 --> 00:43:11.543
to do the deep thoughts
at Christmas Conference,

00:43:11.543 --> 00:43:13.250
because you never told me that.

00:43:13.250 --> 00:43:15.310
No. Never thought that never.

00:43:15.310 --> 00:43:16.480
Okay, good. I'm don't feel bad.

00:43:16.480 --> 00:43:18.052
I ran out of thoughts.

00:43:18.052 --> 00:43:19.710
You ran outta deep thoughts.

00:43:19.710 --> 00:43:21.460
You stopped thinking deeply after college.

00:43:21.460 --> 00:43:25.520
Yeah. I've been writing them
down every week for 22 years.

00:43:25.520 --> 00:43:29.240
Well rewinding a little
bit to that junior year,

00:43:29.240 --> 00:43:33.010
because again, the first time,

00:43:33.010 --> 00:43:35.430
so that was the year we
moved off campus. Right?

00:43:35.430 --> 00:43:37.410
And this is the first time

00:43:37.410 --> 00:43:40.820
that the two of us ever
lived with anyone else.

00:43:40.820 --> 00:43:42.300
So of course we had
lived together freshman

00:43:42.300 --> 00:43:43.940
and sophomore year in our dorm,

00:43:43.940 --> 00:43:46.570
but when we decided to move
off campus to Gorman Crossings,

00:43:46.570 --> 00:43:47.993
apartment 3000C.

00:43:49.020 --> 00:43:50.290
I'm sure it was a no brainer

00:43:50.290 --> 00:43:51.980
that it was gonna be the three of us.

00:43:51.980 --> 00:43:54.280
Yeah. Yeah. That only made sense. Right?

00:43:54.280 --> 00:43:56.350
It was so exciting.

00:43:56.350 --> 00:43:58.427
I remember that moment too of just like,

00:43:58.427 --> 00:44:01.040
"Hey, we're getting an apartment."

00:44:01.040 --> 00:44:03.110
Yeah. It had stairs in it.

00:44:03.110 --> 00:44:03.943
We each-

00:44:03.943 --> 00:44:05.000
No, it didn't. No.

00:44:05.000 --> 00:44:06.581
When we did the tour, it had stairs.

00:44:06.581 --> 00:44:08.170
I remember the tour had stairs in it.

00:44:08.170 --> 00:44:09.600
It was a two story one,

00:44:09.600 --> 00:44:11.840
and then we ended up
getting the one story one

00:44:11.840 --> 00:44:12.673
is what we ended up renting.

00:44:12.673 --> 00:44:16.670
But I was like, this is so
crazy. There's carpet on stairs.

00:44:16.670 --> 00:44:18.290
I don't know why that
was fascinating to me.

00:44:18.290 --> 00:44:19.340
Oh yeah. We didn't get that one.

00:44:19.340 --> 00:44:21.610
We thought that Gorman
Crossings was so nice.

00:44:21.610 --> 00:44:23.200
It was a three bedroom apartment.

00:44:23.200 --> 00:44:24.480
We each had our own bedroom.

00:44:24.480 --> 00:44:27.810
It had a sliding glass
doors off the living room

00:44:27.810 --> 00:44:29.860
that went right into like stoop,

00:44:29.860 --> 00:44:32.390
and I was like, this is our new stoop.

00:44:32.390 --> 00:44:33.640
We now have sunlight.

00:44:33.640 --> 00:44:36.560
We get to sit out here and have
our three hour conversations

00:44:36.560 --> 00:44:38.623
and smoke pipes by George.

00:44:39.890 --> 00:44:43.810
So yeah, playing house
with your best friends.

00:44:43.810 --> 00:44:46.970
It was so much fun. We moved Mr. Fly,

00:44:46.970 --> 00:44:48.470
our couch into there.

00:44:48.470 --> 00:44:52.470
We decorated the room
with the Lionel vinyl

00:44:52.470 --> 00:44:54.850
and the Michael Jackson, Thriller vinyl.

00:44:54.850 --> 00:44:56.700
We found a couch out by the dumpster

00:44:56.700 --> 00:45:00.200
that looked similar to Mr. Fly.

00:45:00.200 --> 00:45:03.388
It was like a golden and green.

00:45:03.388 --> 00:45:04.340
Do you remember that?

00:45:04.340 --> 00:45:06.668
Yep. And we brought in,

00:45:06.668 --> 00:45:10.350
all the furniture was
probably from thrift stores.

00:45:10.350 --> 00:45:11.220
Pretty junky.

00:45:11.220 --> 00:45:14.190
And I remember you had a
thing for a label maker.

00:45:14.190 --> 00:45:17.350
Yeah. Yeah. I labeled things.

00:45:17.350 --> 00:45:19.953
You would label things for comedic effect.

00:45:21.100 --> 00:45:22.450
Yep. Yep. Still do.

00:45:22.450 --> 00:45:26.203
Like our VCR said, "VCR" on it,

00:45:28.060 --> 00:45:31.970
which reminds me of the big God macing

00:45:32.806 --> 00:45:36.280
initiative that I think
I was in a relationship.

00:45:36.280 --> 00:45:37.113
You were in a relationship.

00:45:37.113 --> 00:45:37.946
I was dating Christie.

00:45:37.946 --> 00:45:38.779
Do you remember? Okay.

00:45:38.779 --> 00:45:39.612
I was on the fringe of this.

00:45:39.612 --> 00:45:42.760
Gregg, do you remember the
God macing idea that we had?

00:45:42.760 --> 00:45:46.090
Yeah. I think we had that
idea while we were going out

00:45:46.090 --> 00:45:48.513
the Mission Valley, dollar 50 theater.

00:45:49.360 --> 00:45:52.690
This is where the plan
really got hashed out.

00:45:52.690 --> 00:45:53.523
Yeah.

00:45:53.523 --> 00:45:55.040
But it culminated

00:45:55.040 --> 00:45:58.510
with the Plan of The Apes movie marathon.

00:45:58.510 --> 00:46:01.450
Yeah. So for the uninitiated.

00:46:01.450 --> 00:46:03.300
For those of you who
weren't there that night.

00:46:03.300 --> 00:46:07.200
God macing, it was me and Gregg's idea to,

00:46:07.200 --> 00:46:09.560
and this just goes very much against

00:46:09.560 --> 00:46:11.980
what we were being told by Campus Crusade.

00:46:11.980 --> 00:46:15.120
You find a girl who's not Christian,

00:46:15.120 --> 00:46:17.550
or maybe she's not a serious Christian,

00:46:17.550 --> 00:46:21.130
and you date her with the intention

00:46:21.130 --> 00:46:22.880
that she would become a Christian.

00:46:22.880 --> 00:46:23.963
And really it was,

00:46:24.970 --> 00:46:26.394
I think it was because
we were a little bit--

00:46:26.394 --> 00:46:27.227
That's not good though.

00:46:27.227 --> 00:46:28.960
We were a little bit frustrated
with the pool of women

00:46:28.960 --> 00:46:32.742
that we had at our
disposal in Campus Crusade.

00:46:32.742 --> 00:46:33.575
Disposal.

00:46:33.575 --> 00:46:36.700
We wanted to expand the circle,

00:46:36.700 --> 00:46:38.763
and the only way we could justify it is,

00:46:40.600 --> 00:46:42.200
it was an active evangelism.

00:46:42.200 --> 00:46:44.644
How do you remember the
"Planet of The Apes" idea?

00:46:44.644 --> 00:46:45.477
I don't know.

00:46:45.477 --> 00:46:47.847
Like every good idea
how, who came up with it?

00:46:47.847 --> 00:46:48.680
I don't know.

00:46:48.680 --> 00:46:51.230
But I know that we had multiple TVs,

00:46:51.230 --> 00:46:54.930
and we had a VCR and we figured out a way

00:46:54.930 --> 00:46:59.155
to hook multiple TVs to one VCR.

00:46:59.155 --> 00:47:01.830
Yeah. That's a classic, Gregg.

00:47:01.830 --> 00:47:03.170
There was "Planet of The Apes"

00:47:03.170 --> 00:47:04.590
In every single room in the apartment.

00:47:04.590 --> 00:47:07.713
You created a closed
circuit television system,

00:47:09.140 --> 00:47:14.140
in anticipation for so many
women, who you didn't know.

00:47:15.600 --> 00:47:18.180
We wanted to it to be us and all women.

00:47:18.180 --> 00:47:19.340
That was the plan.

00:47:19.340 --> 00:47:24.340
So you went around campus
and started inviting--

00:47:25.270 --> 00:47:26.480
We would go up to-

00:47:26.480 --> 00:47:27.370
Strange women.

00:47:27.370 --> 00:47:28.493
We would just go up to-

00:47:28.493 --> 00:47:30.170
Well, strangers.
Group of girls talking,

00:47:30.170 --> 00:47:32.203
and be like, "How you guys doing?

00:47:33.867 --> 00:47:36.230
"We're showing "Planet of
The Apes" in our apartment."

00:47:36.230 --> 00:47:37.740
Some people come. Some girls came.

00:47:37.740 --> 00:47:38.982
They did, they showed up.

